Async evaluation awaits both the check and the selected payload. Nested async scopes unwind and perform their existing cancellation/cleanup before the function returns. Work already started concurrently is not rolled back; do not use early return as a transaction or as a replacement for cancellation.

$$a := 1;
$$center := 1/2;
$$view := .Fragment([
    .ControlPanel([
        .Controls.Slider($$a, -5:5, 1, "a"),
        .Controls.Slider($$center, -3:3, 1/2, "center")
    ], "Parameters"),
    .Paragraph(@"a = @{$a}; center = @{$center}")
]);
.Out("index.html", $view)
The target argument must be a $$ identity. Passing a number, expression, or plain $value is rejected because the host would not know what to update.

Prefer replacing a collection with a new value, for example items -&gt; items ++ [entry]. In-place mutations such as Push! do not publish deep reactive changes automatically. After an intentional batch of mutation, call .Touch() on the identity:
$$items := [1];
$$size := $items.Len();
$items.Push!(2);
$$items.Touch();
$size  # 2
.Touch() preserves the current object, starts one reactive epoch, and recomputes its dependent closure. It is available on every reactive node and as graph.Touch("name") for explicit .ReactiveGraph values. It should not be used to hide ordinary replacement state changes.

Renderers are ordinary opt-in plugins. Load one target, load a configured group, or declare the plugins in a script header:
.Plugin.Load("svg");
.Plugin.Load("markdown");

targets := .Renderer.List();
svg := .Renderer.Info("image/svg+xml");
/**
plugins: [terminal-ascii, svg, canvas, tikz, png, markdown, html, quarto, latex, pdf, gif, gltf, csv]
**/
The CLI also accepts --plugins=renderers, and rix setup --plugins=renderers can make the group part of the local CLI configuration. .Renderer.Info(target) reports the canonical target, MIME type, extension, accepted input kinds, aliases, and determinism claim.

The generic and target-specific calls are equivalent:
result := .Render(graphic, "svg", {= alt="An exact construction" });
same := .svg.Render(graphic, {= alt="An exact construction" });
source := result.Get("content");
.Out asks the CLI host to select a loaded renderer from the filename’s longest matching extension. It writes original binary bytes rather than the RiX-visible base64 representation:
.Out("diagram.svg", graphic);
.Out("diagram.canvas.json", graphic);
.Out("report.pdf", report);
Run a program containing those declarations with rix --out=out program.rix. Paths must remain relative to the output directory. Renderer-supplied assets are subject to the same validation.

Public @ratmath/rix and @ratmath/rix/rixcel-workbook exports include:

createRixCelWorkbookDocument(records, externalSnapshots) and parseRixCelWorkbook(dataOrText): inert validation/canonicalization.
stringifyRixCelWorkbook(record): portable JSON, format rixcel-workbook, version 1.
createRixCelWorkbook(record, {compileFormula, runFormula, withEpoch, ...}): execution with an explicitly supplied isolated evaluator.

The Cel host adapter apps/cel/src/workbook-runtime.js supplies the evaluator, cooperative instruction/time budgets and a fresh private context per candidate. It withholds dynamic code/plugin installation, host/IO/async capabilities, random generators and global configuration changes. Volatile values enter only as explicit external snapshots. Merely parsing a workbook does not evaluate it; opening it in the editor explicitly executes its RiX formulas in the worker.
A running workbook exposes:
book.get("report", "total");                // copied exact value
book.edit("sales", {type:"slot:set", index:[1,1], source:"10"});
book.transaction([
  {id:"sales", document:updatedSalesDocument},
  {id:"report", names:{tax:{source:"1/4"}}}
]);
book.sheet("report");                      // live read-only FormulaSheet adapter
book.materialize("report", [[1,1], [2,1]]); // bounded visible cells, one epoch
book.dependencies("report", "total");      // direct dependency paths
book.export();                             // copied portable record, no caches
const unsubscribe = book.subscribe(event =&gt; updateView(event));
unsubscribe();
book.dispose();
Transactions compile and evaluate a private candidate coordinator graph, then replace every document at one commit point. Each computed node runs at most once per epoch; dependencies include cross-document exports/imports and owned names. Cycle errors retain the full path, such as sales.exports.subtotal -&gt; sales.grid[1,1] -&gt; report.exports.total -&gt; ... -&gt; sales.exports.subtotal. Compilation, runtime, cycle and budget failures retain the old values, metadata and epoch. Subscriber exceptions do not undo a committed update. Reentrant mutation during notification is rejected. Returned values and records are copies.
This bounded implementation rebuilds the materialized graph per transaction; it does not claim affected-node-only recomputation or huge-workbook throughput. Implicit cells stay lazy. Batch visible reads with materialize to avoid repeated epochs. Pure runtime callbacks must be synchronous; the Cel adapter enforces the instruction budget. Arbitrary host callbacks cannot be forcibly preempted in-process. The browser worker remains the wall-clock termination boundary.
Slot-target names/exports follow C1’s stable cell identities through structural insertion and undo. Removing an exported newly inserted cell by undo requires changing its definition in the same transaction. A source definition outside the cell grid that mentions grid/near must be explicitly updated in that transaction; its relative intent is never guessed. Arbitrary document replacement through replace(record) uses the explicitly supplied new definitions.

Documentation authority
When pages disagree, use this order:

tests and current implementation;
the generated runtime catalog;
current user guides under eval/, tutorial/, and the complete introduction;
parser and runtime design records;
historical reports, early phase documents, scratch notes, and generated legacy HTML.

The build regenerates the runtime catalog from the implementation, which makes missing or renamed system capabilities visible in every documentation change.

Short glimpses of language features worth exploring. TO ADD MORE.


Validate or destructure a computed value without wrapping it in a function. Soft gates return _—or advance inside {?}—while strict gates stop and throw:
answer := {?
  ReadPrimary() ?- value: [value ? :Integer] ?!- value: [value &gt; 0];
  ReadBackup() ?- {: value, status }: [status == :ready];
  0
}
The first gate also chooses how errors from the candidate expression itself are handled. Each candidate runs once, successful arms return that original value, and all trial bindings stay local.



RiX can store an exact complex number as Cayley(r, t), where r is its nonnegative exact magnitude and t = tan(theta/2) is a stereographic direction coordinate. This avoids transcendental angles while making multiplication, division, integer powers, reciprocal, and conjugation algebraic:
z := .Complex.Cayley(1 + 1~{i});
z                              ## Cayley(sqrt2, sqrt2 - 1)
(z^3).Cartesian()              ## -2 + 2i
The negative real direction uses one projective Infinity point. Addition and subtraction take an exact Cartesian bridge and return to Cayley form. See Exact Cayley Polar Complex Numbers.

Validate or destructure a computed value without wrapping it in a function. Soft gates return _—or advance inside {?}—while strict gates stop and throw:
answer := {?
  ReadPrimary() ?- value: [value ? :Integer] ?!- value: [value &gt; 0];
  ReadBackup() ?- {: value, status }: [status == :ready];
  0
}
The first gate also chooses how errors from the candidate expression itself are handled. Each candidate runs once, successful arms return that original value, and all trial bindings stay local.

Functions can prepare their inputs
Positive(x) ?- [x &gt; 0] -&gt; x;
Nonzero(x) ?!- [x != 0] -&gt; x;
?- is a soft preparation gate: failure means “this function does not accept that candidate.” ?!- is strict and propagates failure. ??- additionally lets undecided fall through; ??!- throws unless the guard is decided and successful. Ordered variants combine into multifunctions, letting dispatch be written in RiX rather than hidden in host code.
For an expected rejection that deserves a result, use check ?_&gt; diagnostic on decided negative/null, or check ??&gt; diagnostic on undecided. These return from the active function call, including from prep or a nested block, rather than selecting another variant. They are errors outside a call. See diagnostic guards and early returns.

Generators can stay lazy
finite := [1, |+ 2, |; 6];
lazy := [1, |+ 2, |^ 6];
firstFive := [1, |+ 1, |^ _][1:5];
Generator clauses describe seeds, recurrence/history, transforms, filters, and stopping. Lazy sequences compute and cache only what a consumer asks for.

Units and exact generators compose
distance := 120~[m];
elapsed := 30~[s] + 2~[min];
speed := .ConvertUnit(distance / elapsed, "m/s");

z := 1 + 1~{i};
c := .Complex.Cayley(z);
c.Cartesian();
Scientific unit sugar (~[...]) resolves through the configurable .Units collection. Mathematical/exact generator sugar (~{...}) resolves through .Exact. Both participate in ordinary arithmetic dispatch.

The system object is explicit
.Len([10, 20, 30]);
.Warn("large interval", {= width = 12 });
.Units[:degC](20);
The leading dot is the system capability object. Hosts can construct and freeze it, add capabilities, or withhold groups for imported scripts. Operator functions are first-class too: @+ names addition, and @+[2] caps it to two incoming arguments.

What RiX is for
RiX is especially interesting when a calculation is more than “feed numbers to a library”:

exact and interval-aware experimentation;
reproducible mathematical notebooks and small scripts;
unit-bearing physical calculations;
symbolic or semantically tagged values;
compact collection and tensor transformations;
research into language semantics for mathematical work;
embedding a configurable mathematical parser and evaluator in another tool.

It is an active alpha, not yet a finished general-purpose ecosystem. The parser, lowering pipeline, and broad evaluator are working and heavily tested; bounded equation solvers and opt-in symbolic rules are available, while general solving and multivariate elimination remain outside the supported scope. See implementation status for the honest boundary.

Embed RiX in JavaScript
import {
  Context,
  createDefaultRegistry,
  createDefaultSystemContext,
  parseAndEvaluate,
} from "@ratmath/rix";

const context = new Context();
const registry = createDefaultRegistry();
const systemContext = createDefaultSystemContext();

const result = parseAndEvaluate("x := 7; x^2 + 1", {
  context,
  registry,
  systemContext,
});
The public package also exports narrower entry points from @ratmath/rix/parser, @ratmath/rix/eval, and @ratmath/rix/runtime. See the developer guide for the pipeline and extension boundaries.

1. A ten-minute start

Exact probability
The probability of exactly five heads in ten fair tosses is a rational number.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
coins := .probability.Binomial(10,1/2);
[coins.PMF(5),coins.CDF(5),coins.Mean(),coins.Variance()];

Output
[63/256, 319/512, 5, 2..1/2]



Describe observed data
Summary computes exact descriptive statistics. Input order does not matter.
.Plugin.Load("stats");
summary := .stats.Summary([8,5,9,7,6]);
[summary[:minimum],summary[:median],summary[:mean],summary[:sampleVariance]];

Output
[5, 7, 7, 2..1/2]



Keep measurement uncertainty
Suppose three distances were recorded only to within stated error bounds. The mean is then a range containing the mean of every point dataset consistent with the measurements.
.Plugin.Load("stats");
distances := [99/10:101/10,119/10:121/10,139/10:141/10];
summary := .stats.IntervalSummary(distances);
[summary[:meanRange],summary[:minimumRange],summary[:maximumRange]];

Output
[11..9/10:12..1/10, 9..9/10:10..1/10, 13..9/10:14..1/10]

RiX does not replace those intervals by their midpoints unless you explicitly ask for the midpoint summary. That prevents a neat-looking point answer from claiming more than the measurements establish.

2. Finite probability

Counting
.Plugin.Load("probability");
[
    .probability.Choose(52,5),
    .probability.Permutations(10,3),
    .probability.MultinomialCoefficient([2,3,1])
];

Output
[2598960, 720, 60]

Use bounded enumeration for genuinely small sample spaces. Here the 36 ordered two-die outcomes make the event “sum equals seven” transparent.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
rolls := .probability.CartesianPower([1,2,3,4,5,6],2);
seven := .probability.Event(rolls,(roll)-&gt;roll[1]+roll[2]==7);
[seven[:favorableCount],seven[:outcomeCount],seven[:probability]];

Output
[6, 36, 1/6]

For larger repeated experiments, use a direct distribution such as Binomial, Multinomial, Dice, or CardDraw rather than materializing the whole sample space.


Conditional probability and Bayes’ rule
Conditional returns another finite distribution. This example conditions a fair die on an even result.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
die := .probability.Finite([1,2,3,4,5,6],[1,1,1,1,1,1]);
even := .probability.Conditional(die,(x)-&gt;x%2==0);
[
    even.PMF(4),
    .probability.ConditionalProbability(die,(x)-&gt;x&gt;=5,(x)-&gt;x%2==0)
];

Output
[1/3, 1/3]

Bayes(prior,likelihood under H,likelihood under not-H) exposes every term of the update. In a screening example with a 1% prior prevalence, 99% sensitivity, and 1% false-positive rate, a positive result gives a posterior of one half.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
update := .probability.Bayes(1/100,99/100,1/100);
[update[:prior],update[:evidence],update[:posterior]];

Output
[1/100, 99/5000, 1/2]



Random variables, joint laws, and moments
A random variable is a function of an outcome. RandomVariable pushes a finite law through such a function; duplicate transformed outcomes have their probabilities combined by the resulting PMF.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
die := .probability.Finite([1,2,3,4,5,6],[1,1,1,1,1,1]);
parity := .probability.RandomVariable(die,(x)-&gt;x%2);
joint := .probability.Joint(die,(x)-&gt;x%2,(x)-&gt;x&gt;=4);
[
    parity.PMF(0),
    .probability.Marginal(joint,2).PMF(1),
    .probability.ExpectedValue(die),
    .probability.VarianceOf(die),
    .probability.Covariance(die,(x)-&gt;x,(x)-&gt;2*x)
];

Output
[1/2, 1/2, 3..1/2, 2..11/12, 5..5/6]

Independent(joint,predicate1,predicate2) returns 1 or 0 by checking the exact product rule. These finite-law tools are excellent for tables, dice, cards, and classroom Bayes exercises. Sums/convolutions of arbitrary user laws and mixtures remain a later probability slice.

3. Standard distributions and simulation
The Phase 1–2 library includes Bernoulli, binomial, multinomial, categorical, geometric, negative-binomial, hypergeometric, Poisson, uniform, exponential, gamma, beta, chi-square, normal, Student-t, F, lognormal, and Cauchy laws. Discrete laws use PMF; continuous laws use PDF. Both use CDF, Quantile, Mean, Variance, Sample, and Simulate where mathematically applicable.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
geometric := .probability.Geometric(1/3);
normal := .probability.Normal(10,2);
cauchy := .probability.Cauchy();
[
    geometric.PMF(2),geometric.CDF(4),
    .numerics.Refine(normal.CDF(10),{= absoluteWidth=1/1000,maxWork=1000 })[:interval],
    cauchy.Mean(),cauchy.Variance(),cauchy.MomentsExist()
];

Output
[4/27, 211/243, 1/2:1/2, _, _, 0]

A simulation record keeps the seed and sampling policy. Repeating the seed replays the experiment.
.Plugin.Load("probability");
dice := .probability.Dice(2,6);
first := dice.Simulate(12,{= seed=2026 });
second := dice.Simulate(12,{= seed=2026 });
[first[:values]==second[:values],first[:samplingPolicy],first[:exactSampling]];

Output
[_, exactUniformDice, 1]

Finite rational sampling is exact. Continuous simulation uses a disclosed finite inverse-CDF grid, so its record says exactSampling=0. This is useful simulation, but it is not mislabeled as an exact draw from a continuum.

4. Typed data tables
Create a relation from a schema and rows. Types are Any, Integer, Rational, Number, Interval, and String; columns are nullable unless nullable=0 is stated.
.Plugin.Load("data");
observations := .data.Relation([
    {= id="student",type=:String,nullable=0 },
    {= id="section",type=:String,nullable=0 },
    {= id="score",type=:Rational }
],[
    ["Ada","A",9/10],
    ["Bo","B",4/5],
    ["Cy","A",_],
    ["Di","B",7/10]
]);
known := .data.Missing(observations,["score"],:drop);
ordered := .data.Sort(known,["score"],{= descending=1 });
.data.TableView(ordered,{= caption="Known scores" });

Output
Known scores
student  section  score
-------  -------  -----
    Ada        A   9/10
     Bo        B    4/5
     Di        B   7/10

Useful cleaning and reshaping operations include Project, Rename, Distinct, Filter, Sort, Join, Calculate, and Missing. Grouped aggregation keeps rational sums and means exact.
.Plugin.Load("data");
scores := .data.Relation([
    {= id="section",type=:String,nullable=0 },
    {= id="score",type=:Rational,nullable=0 }
],[["A",4/5],["A",9/10],["B",7/10],["B",3/4]]);
groups := .data.Group(scores,["section"]);
summary := .data.Aggregate(groups,[
    {= id="n",op=:count },
    {= id="mean",column="score",op=:mean },
    {= id="minimum",column="score",op=:min }
]);
.data.Rows(summary);

Output
[{= section=A, n=2, mean=17/20, minimum=4/5 }, {= section=B, n=2, mean=29/40, minimum=7/10 }]

Frequency produces tidy counts and exact proportions. Contingency produces the count matrix and margins expected by a chi-square independence test.
.Plugin.Load("data");
.Plugin.Load("stats");
survey := .data.Relation([
    {= id="group",type=:String,nullable=0 },
    {= id="answer",type=:String,nullable=0 }
],[["A","yes"],["A","yes"],["A","no"],["B","yes"],["B","no"],["B","no"]]);
frequencies := .data.Frequency(survey,["group","answer"]);
contingency := .data.Contingency(survey,"group","answer");
test := .stats.ChiSquareIndependence(contingency[:counts]);
[.data.Rows(frequencies),contingency[:rowTotals],test[:statistic],test.Decision()[:status]];

Output
[[{= group=A, answer=yes, count=2, proportion=1/3 }, {= group=A, answer=no, count=1, proportion=1/6 }, {= group=B, answer=yes, count=1, proportion=1/6 }, {= group=B, answer=no, count=2, proportion=1/3 }], [3, 3], 2/3, failToReject]

6. Confidence intervals and tests
RiX records the method, reference distribution, assumptions, and p-value status alongside the headline result. It does not claim to have verified that observations are independent or normally distributed.
Common procedures are:

one- and two-sample z tests with known population scales;
one- and two-proportion score z tests;
one-sample, paired, pooled, and Welch t tests;
one-way ANOVA;
Pearson chi-square goodness-of-fit and independence tests;
Pearson correlation and its t test; and
simple linear regression and its slope t test.

.Plugin.Load("stats");
tTest := .stats.OneSampleTTest([12,11,15,14,13],10,:greater);
anova := .stats.OneWayANOVA([[8,9,10],[11,12,13],[9,10,11]]);
goodness := .stats.ChiSquareGoodnessOfFit([20,30,50],[25,25,50]);
[
    tTest[:estimate],tTest[:degreesOfFreedom],tTest.Decision()[:status],
    anova[:statistic],anova.Decision()[:status],
    goodness[:statistic],goodness.Decision()[:status]
];

Output
[13, 4, reject, 7, reject, 2, failToReject]

For a confidence interval using Student’s t reference, use MeanTConfidence; related operations cover paired and independent mean differences. The critical-value search returns an outward bracket and uses its upper endpoint, making the reported interval conservatively wide.
.Plugin.Load("stats");
confidence := .stats.MeanTConfidence([1,2,3,4,5],9/10,{=
    extent=10,
    criticalWidth=1/100,
    probabilityWidth=1/10000,
    maxWork=600,
    maxIterations=16
});
[
    confidence[:estimate],confidence[:degreesOfFreedom],
    confidence[:criticalValueInterval],confidence[:criticalPolicy]
];

Output
[3, 4, 2..33/256:2..71/512, outwardUpper]

TestDecision(alpha) refines a certified p-value. It returns :unresolved instead of guessing if the enclosure still crosses alpha. “Fail to reject” is not evidence that the null hypothesis is true.

7. Correlation and regression
.Plugin.Load("stats");
x := [1,2,3,4,5];
y := [2,3,5,4,7];
model := .stats.LinearRegression(x,y);
slopeTest := .stats.RegressionSlopeTest(model);
[
    .stats.Correlation(x,y),
    model[:intercept],model[:slope],model[:rSquared],
    .stats.Predict(model,6),slopeTest[:test]
